---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RadiusCell from '../components/RadiusCell.astro';
import {
  YIELDS,
  EFFECT_ROWS,
  get,
  crossChecks,
  fmtFt,
  manual,
} from '../lib/effects';

const sub: Record<string, string> = {
  fireball: 'breakaway radius · Eq. (2.127.1)',
  overpressure_5psi: 'peak of the 4 and 6 psi contours, interpolated',
  overpressure_1psi: 'peak of the outermost contour',
  thermal_2nd: '4.5 cal/cm² · light skin, unshaded',
  thermal_3rd: '10 cal/cm² · light skin, unshaded',
};

const groundRows = EFFECT_ROWS.filter(
  (r) => r.id === 'fireball' || r.id.startsWith('overpressure'),
);

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: 'Nuclear weapon blast radius, 15 kt to 1 Mt',
  description:
    'Fireball, 5 psi, 1 psi and thermal radii for 15, 100 and 1000 kt, transcribed from The Effects of Nuclear Weapons (Glasstone & Dolan, 3rd ed., 1977) and scaled only by the cube-root law printed in that manual.',
  url: 'https://apocalab.com/blast-radius/',
  license: 'https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/',
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'apocalab' },
  isBasedOn: {
    '@type': 'Book',
    name: 'The Effects of Nuclear Weapons',
    edition: '3rd',
    datePublished: '1977',
    identifier: 'doi:10.2172/6852629',
    publisher: 'U.S. Department of Defense / ERDA',
  },
  variableMeasured: [
    'fireball radius',
    '5 psi overpressure radius',
    '1 psi overpressure radius',
    'second-degree burn radius',
    'third-degree burn radius',
  ],
};
---

<Base
  title="Nuclear Bomb Blast Radius Table: 15 kt, 100 kt, 1 Mt"
  description="Three yields, four effects: every radius in the table shows the printed page it came from, and whether it was read off a chart or scaled from one."
  path="/blast-radius/"
  current="blast-radius"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">
    {manual.title} · {manual.edition} edition, {manual.year} · public domain
  </p>
  <h1>Nuclear Bomb Blast Radius, with the printed page behind every number</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The table gives the distance from ground zero to four effects, at three yields. None of it
    is model output: the blast radii are read off Fig.&nbsp;3.73c at 1 kt and then scaled by the
    manual's own cube-root law (Eq.&nbsp;3.61.2, p.&nbsp;101); the thermal radii are read
    directly off Fig.&nbsp;7.42 (p.&nbsp;291) and are not scaled at all. Every cell carries the
    figure and printed page it came from, so any single number can be checked against the book in
    under a minute.
  </p>

  <h2 id="air">Air burst — the default reading</h2>
  <p>
    Height of burst is not chosen here. Each contour is read at its own peak — the point where it
    reaches furthest from ground zero — so every overpressure carries the burst height that the
    chart itself associates with that maximum. The manual works its own example the same way on
    p.&nbsp;114.
  </p>

  <p class="tbl-hint">Scroll the table sideways →</p>
  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <caption>Radius to each contour, in kilometres. Air burst.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Effect</th>
          {YIELDS.map((y) => <th scope="col">{y.toLocaleString('en-US')} kt</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          EFFECT_ROWS.map((row) => (
            <tr id={row.anchor}>
              <th scope="row">
                {row.name}
                <span class="th-sub">{sub[row.id]}</span>
              </th>
              {YIELDS.map((y) => (
                <RadiusCell entry={get(row.id, 'air', y)} yieldKt={y} />
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note note--warn" id="hob-boundary">
    <strong>Why three cells carry an amber warning.</strong>
    Reading at the peak of each isobar puts the 1 Mt burst heights at 10,174&nbsp;ft (5 psi) and
    15,750&nbsp;ft (1 psi); even the 100 kt 1 psi row lands at 7,311&nbsp;ft.
    Fig.&nbsp;3.73c is computed for a homogeneous sea-level atmosphere at burst heights of
    5,000&nbsp;ft or less (§3.73, p.&nbsp;110), so these cells extrapolate that assumption by up to
    a factor of three. The numbers are still the manual's curve multiplied by the manual's scaling
    law, but they should be read as the edge of what this chart supports rather than as precise
    distances. No altitude correction from §3.64–3.66 is applied here, and leaving it out pushes
    these figures low rather than high.
  </p>

  <p class="note" id="tags">
    <strong>How to read the tags under each number.</strong>
    <code>scaled from the 1 kt curve (§3.61, p.101)</code> means the figure is the 1 kt reading
    multiplied by W<sup>1/3</sup> — the manual's own scaling law, applied once at build time, not
    recomputed in your browser. <code>interpolated between 4 and 6 psi contours</code> means the
    contour does not exist on the chart at all: Fig.&nbsp;3.73c prints 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10 psi
    only, so 5 psi is placed between the 4 and 6 psi curves at the logarithmic pressure position
    0.5503. <code>read directly off Fig. 7.42</code> means no scaling was used. A star (*) marks a
    value that has not been individually spot-checked; see
    <a href="/methodology/#verification">verification</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ground">Contact surface burst</h2>
  <p>
    Height of burst is zero: each distance is read where the contour meets the bottom edge of
    Fig.&nbsp;3.73c, which is the convention the manual states on p.&nbsp;106. A contact burst puts
    the fireball at the surface, so the fireball column switches to Eq.&nbsp;(2.127.2) on
    p.&nbsp;71 — the two fireball coefficients are never mixed.
  </p>

  <p class="tbl-hint">Scroll the table sideways →</p>
  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <caption>Contact surface burst — height of burst = 0. Blast and fireball only.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Effect</th>
          {YIELDS.map((y) => <th scope="col">{y.toLocaleString('en-US')} kt</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          groundRows.map((row) => (
            <tr id={`ground-${row.anchor}`}>
              <th scope="row">
                {row.name}
                <span class="th-sub">
                  {row.id === 'fireball'
                    ? 'contact burst · Eq. (2.127.2)'
                    : 'bottom edge of Fig. 3.73c, height of burst = 0'}
                </span>
              </th>
              {YIELDS.map((y) => (
                <RadiusCell entry={get(row.id, 'ground', y)} yieldKt={y} />
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note">
    <strong>The thermal rows are deliberately absent from this table.</strong>
    Fig.&nbsp;7.42 is drawn for air bursts at burst heights of 200·W<sup>0.4</sup>&nbsp;ft, and the
    manual treats thermal radiation from a surface burst by a separate route (§7.41, p.&nbsp;290).
    Rather than blend two procedures into one column and call the result a number, this page reports
    blast and fireball only for contact bursts. Nothing is missing by accident.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what">What each ring is</h2>

  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Fireball</h3>
      <p>
        The incandescent sphere itself. The value is the breakaway radius — the radius at the moment
        the shock front separates from the fireball. For air bursts it comes from Eq.&nbsp;(2.127.1)
        on p.&nbsp;70; for contact bursts, Eq.&nbsp;(2.127.2) on p.&nbsp;71. §2.129 notes that the
        fireball may grow to about twice this radius, which is why the figure is described as a
        breakaway value rather than a maximum.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>5 psi overpressure</h3>
      <p>
        Peak overpressure on the ground, not the incident pressure in free air. Fig.&nbsp;3.73c
        plots that quantity, so the reflected wave is already inside the number. There is no 5 psi
        contour printed on the chart; this row is interpolated between the 4 and 6 psi curves at a
        fixed logarithmic position, and every 5 psi cell says so.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>1 psi overpressure</h3>
      <p>
        The outermost contour drawn on Fig.&nbsp;3.73c, and the one most sensitive to burst height:
        at 1 kt it reaches 7,020&nbsp;ft at a burst height of 1,575&nbsp;ft, against
        3,830&nbsp;ft for a contact burst. That sensitivity is why this page reads the peak of the
        curve instead of picking a height of burst in advance.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Thermal · second and third degree</h3>
      <p>
        Slant range to a given irradiance, read off a chart drawn for unshaded light skin with
        12-mile visibility. 4.5&nbsp;cal/cm² is the lower edge of the band the manual ties to
        second-degree burns (§12.65, p.&nbsp;563); 10&nbsp;cal/cm² is the column reported for
        third-degree burns (Fig.&nbsp;12.64, p.&nbsp;564). Clothing, shelter, haze and skin tone all
        move this figure, and the page makes no adjustment for any of them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="checks">Cross-checks against the manual's own prose</h2>
  <p>
    Four places where the book states a number in words. If a reading were wrong, these are the
    places it would show up first.
  </p>

  <p class="tbl-hint">Scroll the table sideways →</p>
  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="data">
      <caption>Digitised reading against the sentence in the manual.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Check</th>
          <th scope="col">What the manual says</th>
          <th scope="col">This table</th>
          <th scope="col">Difference</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          crossChecks.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                {c.claim}
                <span class="th-sub">{c.id}</span>
              </th>
              <td>
                {c.manual}
              </td>
              <td>
                <span class="v">
                  {c.ours_mi !== undefined ? `${c.ours_mi} mi` : `${fmtFt(c.ours_ft ?? 0)} ft`}
                </span>
              </td>
              <td>
                <span class="v">{c.delta_pct > 0 ? '+' : ''}{c.delta_pct.toFixed(1)}%</span>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="limits">What this table is not</h2>
  <p>
    It is a set of distances from ground zero, and nothing more. It does not estimate deaths,
    injuries, building damage at a specific address, fallout, or what would happen in any actual
    event. It also does not cover underwater, underground or high-altitude bursts, multiple
    warheads, or terrain shielding. The <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> lists what is
    left out and why, including how the two thermal thresholds were placed and where the 1 Mt rows
    leave the chart's assumptions behind.
  </p>
  <p>
    To reproduce any value: open the cited figure at the cited printed page, read the distance at
    the stated burst height, multiply by W<sup>1/3</sup> where the cell says
    <code>scaled</code>, and convert feet to metres with 0.3048. The same numbers, with the pixel
    coordinates they were read at, ship in
    <code>src/data/effects.json</code> and <code>data-ledger.csv</code>.
  </p>
</Base>
